WEBVTT

0
00:00.170 --> 00:01.660
<v Krista>In this video, we're talking about how to find</v>

1
00:01.660 --> 00:04.400
the greatest common factor of a few monomials.

2
00:04.400 --> 00:06.080
And we've been given three examples.

3
00:06.080 --> 00:07.440
In the first example, we've been asked

4
00:07.440 --> 00:09.220
to find the greatest common factor

5
00:09.220 --> 00:12.170
of our three monomials, five, 10, and 20.

6
00:12.170 --> 00:14.650
And let's just go ahead and clarify a few terms here.

7
00:14.650 --> 00:17.410
Monomial, remember, means just one number.

8
00:17.410 --> 00:19.920
Mono meaning one, and nomial meaning number,

9
00:19.920 --> 00:21.910
so monomial is one number.

10
00:21.910 --> 00:25.610
So, five is one number, 10 is one number, 20 is one number.

11
00:25.610 --> 00:27.440
So these are three monomials.

12
00:27.440 --> 00:28.419
And we've been asked to find

13
00:28.419 --> 00:32.370
the greatest common factor among these three monomials.

14
00:32.370 --> 00:34.980
The greatest common factor is going to be the largest number,

15
00:34.980 --> 00:36.740
or the largest number of factors,

16
00:36.740 --> 00:39.880
that divide evenly into all three of our monomials.

17
00:39.880 --> 00:42.800
So what we want to do to find the greatest common factor

18
00:42.800 --> 00:45.610
at its most basic level, and we'll get faster at doing this

19
00:45.610 --> 00:47.730
as we get more practice, but what we want to do

20
00:47.730 --> 00:50.580
is break down each of our numbers into their prime factors.

21
00:50.580 --> 00:52.760
Then we'll all of the factors that these numbers

22
00:52.760 --> 00:55.020
have in common, and multiply them together

23
00:55.020 --> 00:56.620
to find the greatest common factor.

24
00:56.620 --> 00:58.270
So here's what that looks like.

25
00:58.270 --> 01:00.660
Five broken down into its prime factors

26
01:00.660 --> 01:02.640
is just going to be five, right.

27
01:02.640 --> 01:04.007
Five is already a prime number,

28
01:04.007 --> 01:07.620
so the only factor of five is going to be five.

29
01:07.620 --> 01:09.860
10 broken down into its prime factors

30
01:09.860 --> 01:12.750
is going to be five and two.

31
01:12.750 --> 01:14.930
20 broken down into its prime factors,

32
01:14.930 --> 01:17.890
if we do first, 10 and two,

33
01:17.890 --> 01:20.386
and then from 10 we do five and two,

34
01:20.386 --> 01:25.180
notice that we have five, two, and two.

35
01:25.180 --> 01:27.559
With our monomials broken down into their prime factors,

36
01:27.559 --> 01:29.909
at this point, we're looking for the factors

37
01:29.909 --> 01:33.000
that are common to all three of our monomials.

38
01:33.000 --> 01:34.560
So if we start at the top, and we look at

39
01:34.560 --> 01:36.750
this first factor here of five, right.

40
01:36.750 --> 01:38.870
So we'll look at five here.

41
01:38.870 --> 01:42.140
Is five in our other two monomial numbers?

42
01:42.140 --> 01:42.973
Yes, it is.

43
01:42.973 --> 01:45.720
We have a factor of five here, and a factor of five here.

44
01:45.720 --> 01:48.280
So because this factor of five sort of matches

45
01:48.280 --> 01:49.610
across all three monomials,

46
01:49.610 --> 01:51.910
it exists in the breakdown of all three,

47
01:51.910 --> 01:54.270
we can include it in our greatest common factor.

48
01:54.270 --> 01:56.670
If we look at this next factor here of two,

49
01:56.670 --> 01:58.270
we can see that we have this factor of two

50
01:58.270 --> 02:00.160
involved in the breakdown for 10,

51
02:00.160 --> 02:03.670
and also a factor of two involved in the breakdown for 20,

52
02:03.670 --> 02:05.340
but we don't have a factor of two

53
02:05.340 --> 02:08.890
involved in the breakdown for five, so we can't include it

54
02:08.890 --> 02:10.720
in our greatest common factor.

55
02:10.720 --> 02:12.910
Nor can we include this sort of second two

56
02:12.910 --> 02:15.160
that's involved here in the 20, because again,

57
02:15.160 --> 02:17.090
we still don't have a factor of two

58
02:17.090 --> 02:19.200
involved in our breakdown for five.

59
02:19.200 --> 02:20.940
Which means that the only thing we can include

60
02:20.940 --> 02:23.160
in our greatest common factor is five.

61
02:23.160 --> 02:25.330
So, the greatest common factor for

62
02:25.330 --> 02:27.810
five, 10, and 20 is going to be five.

63
02:27.810 --> 02:29.840
Which means, this is the largest value

64
02:29.840 --> 02:32.810
that can go evenly into five, 10, and 20.

65
02:32.810 --> 02:34.050
Let's go ahead and look at a slightly

66
02:34.050 --> 02:35.930
more complicated example here.

67
02:35.930 --> 02:37.750
We have the three monomial numbers

68
02:37.750 --> 02:41.060
6a, 6a squared, 12a cubed.

69
02:41.060 --> 02:43.520
So we've gotten now a variable involved of a.

70
02:43.520 --> 02:45.100
We need to break these monomials down,

71
02:45.100 --> 02:47.220
first by looking at these constant coefficients.

72
02:47.220 --> 02:49.400
So first we'll look at six, six, and 12,

73
02:49.400 --> 02:51.250
then we'll look at the variables for a.

74
02:51.250 --> 02:54.970
So, if we break down six into its prime factors,

75
02:54.970 --> 02:57.340
the factors are going to be three and two.

76
02:57.340 --> 03:00.010
If we break down six here into its prime factors,

77
03:00.010 --> 03:01.800
we're going to get three and two.

78
03:01.800 --> 03:04.247
If we break down 12 into its prime factors,

79
03:04.247 --> 03:06.250
we'll get four and three.

80
03:06.250 --> 03:08.790
And then break down four into two and two.

81
03:08.790 --> 03:12.660
So what we'll end up with is three, two, and two.

82
03:12.660 --> 03:14.800
Now we need to look at our a variables here.

83
03:14.800 --> 03:16.620
We have just a single factor of a,

84
03:16.620 --> 03:18.480
we can't break that down any further.

85
03:18.480 --> 03:20.250
So we have this factor of a.

86
03:20.250 --> 03:21.490
Here we have a squared,

87
03:21.490 --> 03:24.450
which we can break down into a and a.

88
03:24.450 --> 03:27.070
And here we have a cubed, or three factors of a,

89
03:27.070 --> 03:30.340
so we can break that down into a, a, and a.

90
03:30.340 --> 03:32.230
So now, our monomials are completely

91
03:32.230 --> 03:34.080
factored into their prime factors,

92
03:34.080 --> 03:36.780
and we need to go ahead and look for common factors.

93
03:36.780 --> 03:40.020
So if we start with this first factor in 6a, we have three.

94
03:40.020 --> 03:43.280
And we can see that we have three, three, and three,

95
03:43.280 --> 03:46.270
that factor is common to all three of our monomials.

96
03:46.270 --> 03:50.230
We can see also that two is a factor which is common

97
03:50.230 --> 03:52.570
to all three monomials, so we can pull that out.

98
03:52.570 --> 03:55.210
We have this extra factor of two for the 12,

99
03:55.210 --> 03:57.210
but we don't have that extra factor of two

100
03:57.210 --> 03:59.300
involved in either of these first two monomials,

101
03:59.300 --> 04:01.300
so we're not going to be able to pull that out.

102
04:01.300 --> 04:03.280
So we're going to go ahead and skip to a.

103
04:03.280 --> 04:04.950
We notice that we have a common factor

104
04:04.950 --> 04:08.500
of a in all three monomials.

105
04:08.500 --> 04:10.390
But we don't have a second common factor

106
04:10.390 --> 04:12.450
of a or a third common factor of a,

107
04:12.450 --> 04:14.530
because even though there's an additional a

108
04:14.530 --> 04:17.110
involved in the 6a squared and the 12a cubed,

109
04:17.110 --> 04:20.740
we don't have another factor of a to pull out of 6a.

110
04:20.740 --> 04:22.270
So that means that all we can take

111
04:22.270 --> 04:26.530
is the three, the two, and the a.

112
04:26.530 --> 04:28.350
So we can pull those three things out,

113
04:28.350 --> 04:31.460
and then what we're going to do is multiple them together.

114
04:31.460 --> 04:34.370
And when we do that, we're going to get three times two is six,

115
04:34.370 --> 04:39.250
times a is 6a, so we get 6a as our greatest common factor.

116
04:39.250 --> 04:41.040
If we look at our last example here, we're going to do

117
04:41.040 --> 04:43.320
the same thing that we did in the second example.

118
04:43.320 --> 04:44.400
In other words, we're going to look at

119
04:44.400 --> 04:47.150
these constant coefficients, four, two, and 10,

120
04:47.150 --> 04:49.280
and break those down into their prime factors,

121
04:49.280 --> 04:50.740
and then look at the variables.

122
04:50.740 --> 04:51.930
So for four, two, and 10,

123
04:51.930 --> 04:55.940
we'll break down four into two and two.

124
04:55.940 --> 04:58.380
We'll break down two into just two.

125
04:58.380 --> 05:02.420
And we'll break down 10 into five and two.

126
05:02.420 --> 05:03.900
Then we need to look at our xs.

127
05:03.900 --> 05:05.700
So we have x squared, x, and x.

128
05:05.700 --> 05:09.680
Well, we can break down x squared into x and x.

129
05:09.680 --> 05:11.210
But we can't break down x at all,

130
05:11.210 --> 05:14.060
so we just have x here, and we have x here.

131
05:14.060 --> 05:16.400
And then for y, we can't break that down at all.

132
05:16.400 --> 05:17.980
Can't break it down at all here.

133
05:17.980 --> 05:21.860
And for y squared, we can break that into y and y.

134
05:21.860 --> 05:23.260
So now we've got our prime factors

135
05:23.260 --> 05:25.630
and we need to look for common factors.

136
05:25.630 --> 05:29.100
Notice here that we can pull out a two from each of these.

137
05:29.100 --> 05:30.720
So we'll pull out the two.

138
05:30.720 --> 05:32.610
We can't pull out a five, because five

139
05:32.610 --> 05:35.240
is unique to this 10xy squared.

140
05:35.240 --> 05:36.710
Five doesn't exist as a factor

141
05:36.710 --> 05:38.700
in either of these two monomials.

142
05:38.700 --> 05:40.850
We can't pull out this additional two.

143
05:40.850 --> 05:44.040
But we can pull out an x from all three.

144
05:44.040 --> 05:45.840
So pull out an x from all three,

145
05:45.840 --> 05:47.200
and we'll get that down there.

146
05:47.200 --> 05:49.240
And we can pull out a y from all three.

147
05:49.240 --> 05:52.590
So there's a y, a y, and a y.

148
05:52.590 --> 05:54.060
And we'll pull that out there.

149
05:54.060 --> 05:56.440
We know that we can't possibly take out anything else,

150
05:56.440 --> 05:59.930
because notice here, this 2xy, we've taken every factor

151
05:59.930 --> 06:02.420
from the 2xy, there's no more factors we can take.

152
06:02.420 --> 06:04.500
So it would be literally impossible for us

153
06:04.500 --> 06:07.340
to pull any other factor into our greatest common factor.

154
06:07.340 --> 06:09.710
So then we just multiply our results together,

155
06:09.710 --> 06:13.874
and we say that 2xy is the greatest common factor of

156
06:13.874 --> 06:18.660
4x squared y, 2xy, and 10xy squared.

157
06:18.660 --> 06:19.500
Now I want to make sure that

158
06:19.500 --> 06:21.200
we be careful here with this video,

159
06:21.200 --> 06:24.050
because in these three examples, we took out a five,

160
06:24.050 --> 06:26.810
which happened to be our smallest number.

161
06:26.810 --> 06:28.940
We took out a 6a, which, in a way,

162
06:28.940 --> 06:30.800
happened to be our smallest number here.

163
06:30.800 --> 06:33.120
And we took out a 2xy, which happened

164
06:33.120 --> 06:35.190
to be sort of our smallest number.

165
06:35.190 --> 06:36.640
But that won't always be the case.

166
06:36.640 --> 06:39.260
It won't always work out that the smallest factor

167
06:39.260 --> 06:41.800
that you have here is going to be your greatest common factor.

168
06:41.800 --> 06:43.360
And we can illustrate that by changing

169
06:43.360 --> 06:47.860
this 2xy here to, for example, 2xy squared.

170
06:47.860 --> 06:49.110
If we had been given these three

171
06:49.110 --> 06:52.030
monomials instead, where this was 2xy squared,

172
06:52.030 --> 06:55.170
we only would've been able to pull out a single factor of y,

173
06:55.170 --> 06:56.820
because we would've been limited by the fact

174
06:56.820 --> 06:58.900
that we only have one factor of y here.

175
06:58.900 --> 07:01.090
But we had two of them here, and two of them here.

176
07:01.090 --> 07:05.040
So our greatest common factor still would've been 2xy,

177
07:05.040 --> 07:06.770
but that wouldn't have been the same

178
07:06.770 --> 07:10.580
as sort of our smallest factor 2xy squared here.

179
07:10.580 --> 07:12.000
So we have to be really careful whenever

180
07:12.000 --> 07:13.680
we're finding a greatest common factor,

181
07:13.680 --> 07:16.590
to break each monomial down into its prime factors

182
07:16.590 --> 07:18.890
for the whole numbers or the constant coefficients

183
07:18.890 --> 07:20.740
and for each of our variables.

184
07:20.740 --> 07:22.520
And then make sure that we're really deliberate

185
07:22.520 --> 07:24.680
when we're looking for common factors

186
07:24.680 --> 07:26.963
to build into our greatest common factor.